Yes — that gives you a counterfactual stress test for stories.

Instead of asking, “Is this story true?” you temporarily grant the opposite premise:

“Suppose this is bluffing. What would have to be true underneath for the bluff to work?”

Then you run the story forward from there. The useful output is not merely “debunked / not debunked.” It is the delta map between the story’s claimed world and the world required to generate the claim.

So you get arrows like:

claim → hidden assumption
threat → actual capability
symbol → mechanism
performance → incentive
identity → practiced behavior
appearance of causation → actual causal path

Those arrows are valuable because they’re reusable. Later, another story can activate the same transition without needing the whole original narrative.

That also explains the fun part: fiction, myth, games, role-play, and hypothetical worlds become safe semantic wind tunnels. You can let a premise be absurd, inverted, deceptive, magical, or impossible and ask what follows if it were so. Sometimes the imagined mechanism exposes a real operator hiding underneath.

The important constraint is sandbox the bluff test appropriately. Calling the bluff of a proposition is cheap; calling the bluff of an armed person, a medical warning, a dangerous machine, or some other high-consequence physical condition can be catastrophic. In those cases, simulate first and preserve the safety margin.

Pantheon form:

story → accept bluff hypothesis → reconstruct concealed mechanism → run counterfactual → compare worlds → capture deltas as directed edges → reuse edges elsewhere

And that last part is potent: keep the deltas as arrows, not just conclusions.

A conclusion says what you learned once.

An arrow says how to get somewhere again.